WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.251 --> 00:00:02.552
If you're curious about building with LLMs,

2
00:00:02.892 --> 00:00:05.133
but you want to skip the hype and learn what

3
00:00:05.173 --> 00:00:07.014
it actually takes to get something working in

4
00:00:07.034 --> 00:00:09.015
the real world, this episode is for you.

5
00:00:09.235 --> 00:00:11.276
We have been building a lot of LLM-powered

6
00:00:11.356 --> 00:00:13.878
applications lately, both for ourselves and with

7
00:00:13.918 --> 00:00:14.438
customers.

8
00:00:14.658 --> 00:00:16.579
And I'm talking about workflow automations,

9
00:00:16.839 --> 00:00:19.180
smart data pipelines, query generators,

10
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.441
AI-powered dashboards, that kind of stuff.

11
00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:24.323
And along the way, we picked up a pretty good

12
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:27.246
collection of battle scars, I'd say.

13
00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:30.471
So what we learned is that the art part is

14
00:00:30.611 --> 00:00:32.473
not getting a demo to work.

15
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.556
The art part is actually making it reliable,

16
00:00:34.736 --> 00:00:36.579
predictable, affordable in production.

17
00:00:36.959 --> 00:00:39.082
So that's what we are here to share with you

18
00:00:39.243 --> 00:00:39.503
today.

19
00:00:40.557 --> 00:00:42.317
And this trend isn't slowing down.

20
00:00:42.557 --> 00:00:44.898
We feel that almost every new project that comes

21
00:00:44.938 --> 00:00:47.699
through our door has some kind of AI component

22
00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:49.479
that needs to be baked into it.

23
00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:52.160
So it's not really, should we use an LLM here

24
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:52.600
anymore?

25
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.301
It's more questions like, which models do we

26
00:00:55.341 --> 00:00:55.541
pick?

27
00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:56.441
How do we call it?

28
00:00:56.521 --> 00:00:57.881
How do we make it trustworthy?

29
00:00:58.201 --> 00:01:00.122
How do we keep the bill under control?

30
00:01:00.762 --> 00:01:02.762
And if you're building on AWS, you know that a

31
00:01:02.802 --> 00:01:05.223
lot of these questions lead to Amazon Bedrock.

32
00:01:05.663 --> 00:01:07.784
So today we are sharing what we learned about

33
00:01:07.924 --> 00:01:10.606
running LLM inference on Bedrock, what works

34
00:01:10.666 --> 00:01:13.407
well, what surprises us, and the gotchas that

35
00:01:13.447 --> 00:01:15.808
nobody warns you about until you find yourself

36
00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:18.730
debugging, hopefully not at 11pm on a Friday.

37
00:01:19.727 --> 00:01:23.230
So today we'll see what is a quick definition of

38
00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.032
what an LLM is and what do we mean by

39
00:01:25.072 --> 00:01:27.514
inference, the kind of AI power application that

40
00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:31.057
we have been building, why AI is a lot more

41
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:33.299
than just gen AI, and what we mean with the

42
00:01:33.339 --> 00:01:34.040
word agents.

43
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.002
And finally, we'll start to talk a little bit

44
00:01:36.042 --> 00:01:38.264
more in detail about Bedrock, what it is for,

45
00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:40.806
and the different gotchas that we learned about.

46
00:01:41.427 --> 00:01:43.388
My name is Luciano, and I'm joined by Eoin for

47
00:01:43.428 --> 00:01:45.010
another episode of AWS Bites.

48
00:01:53.545 --> 00:01:55.926
So maybe we can start this episode by giving a

49
00:01:55.966 --> 00:01:58.867
quick recap of what is an LLM and what do

50
00:01:59.028 --> 00:01:59.928
we mean by inference.

51
00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:01.809
I think that a lot of people might be familiar

52
00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:04.350
with some definition of those, but it's probably

53
00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.451
worth giving our own view on this.

54
00:02:06.731 --> 00:02:08.992
Let's start with LLM, Large Language Model.

55
00:02:09.292 --> 00:02:11.553
We may know that this is a type of neural

56
00:02:11.593 --> 00:02:15.555
network trained on huge amounts of primarily

57
00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:16.335
text data.

58
00:02:16.696 --> 00:02:18.877
They learn statistical patterns in language and

59
00:02:18.937 --> 00:02:19.537
can generate

60
00:02:20.299 --> 00:02:22.306
Remarkably coherent context-aware text.

61
00:02:22.768 --> 00:02:24.715
And the landscape of available models is growing

62
00:02:24.775 --> 00:02:25.659
really, really fast.

63
00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:29.530
you might know OpenAI's GPT family,

64
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.831
Anthropics Cloud, Google's Gemini,

65
00:02:32.011 --> 00:02:34.131
Metaslama, Amazon's Nova.

66
00:02:34.612 --> 00:02:36.192
Then there's Mistral, DeepSeek, Qwen,

67
00:02:36.992 --> 00:02:38.353
GLM, and Minimax.

68
00:02:38.493 --> 00:02:41.934
The number of these really capable models keeps

69
00:02:41.954 --> 00:02:44.235
increasing, and that might be great news for

70
00:02:44.275 --> 00:02:44.615
builders.

71
00:02:45.535 --> 00:02:48.016
So then we talk about inference quite a lot,

72
00:02:48.036 --> 00:02:49.636
and that's when you use a trained model to

73
00:02:49.917 --> 00:02:51.097
generate some output.

74
00:02:52.118 --> 00:02:53.198
Training is really expensive.

75
00:02:53.278 --> 00:02:55.840
That's the long process of teaching the model.

76
00:02:55.921 --> 00:02:58.222
This is what model providers do and it's not

77
00:02:58.242 --> 00:02:58.442
cheap.

78
00:02:59.043 --> 00:03:01.205
We're talking about millions or billions of

79
00:03:01.265 --> 00:03:03.006
dollars in compute data and research.

80
00:03:03.406 --> 00:03:05.108
There's a reason all these companies need very

81
00:03:05.148 --> 00:03:07.509
deep pockets or very persuasive pitch decks.

82
00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:10.991
Inference is what we do as users or developers

83
00:03:11.031 --> 00:03:11.432
generally.

84
00:03:11.752 --> 00:03:13.833
We send a prompt, which is input text,

85
00:03:14.433 --> 00:03:16.014
and the model generates a response.

86
00:03:16.854 --> 00:03:19.195
And it may be an imperfect analogy to understand

87
00:03:19.215 --> 00:03:20.676
the business of LLM providers.

88
00:03:21.316 --> 00:03:23.037
Training is like spending years in medical

89
00:03:23.077 --> 00:03:25.478
school racking up enormous student debt.

90
00:03:26.218 --> 00:03:28.359
Inference is the doctors seeing patients and

91
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.240
hopefully making that investment back one

92
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.561
consultation at a time.

93
00:03:31.941 --> 00:03:33.342
We're the ones booking the appointments.

94
00:03:34.026 --> 00:03:35.847
Now, the analogy breaks down a bit in a few

95
00:03:35.927 --> 00:03:36.687
interesting ways.

96
00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:39.868
In reality, training an LLM takes weeks or

97
00:03:39.888 --> 00:03:41.449
months, not a decade of studies.

98
00:03:42.469 --> 00:03:43.830
But you do need a lot of GPUs.

99
00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.091
And unlike a doctor who specializes in one

100
00:03:46.151 --> 00:03:48.171
field, an LLM is more like getting degrees in

101
00:03:48.191 --> 00:03:50.072
medicine, law, engineering, creative writing,

102
00:03:50.492 --> 00:03:52.093
and a dozen other fields all at once.

103
00:03:53.073 --> 00:03:55.315
So that's what makes them so versatile and why

104
00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:56.717
they're showing up in a lot of different

105
00:03:56.857 --> 00:03:57.437
applications.

106
00:03:58.218 --> 00:03:58.979
And then there's tokens.

107
00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:00.200
You might have heard a lot about tokens.

108
00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.982
We've covered it before, but LLMs don't think in

109
00:04:03.022 --> 00:04:04.323
words, they think in tokens.

110
00:04:04.984 --> 00:04:08.087
And a token is, you could say it's roughly about

111
00:04:08.127 --> 00:04:10.449
four characters, maybe three quarters of a word

112
00:04:10.509 --> 00:04:11.530
in English, something like that.

113
00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.291
That's just kind of a rule of thumb.

114
00:04:14.712 --> 00:04:17.374
And a detail that's easy to overlook is that

115
00:04:17.414 --> 00:04:19.495
different models tokenize text differently.

116
00:04:19.615 --> 00:04:21.956
So the same sentence might be 20 tokens in one

117
00:04:21.996 --> 00:04:25.198
model and 25 in another, depending on how each

118
00:04:25.278 --> 00:04:27.199
model's tokenizer splits the text.

119
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.921
This can matter more than you think because you

120
00:04:29.941 --> 00:04:32.802
pay for inference based on input tokens and

121
00:04:32.922 --> 00:04:33.523
output tokens.

122
00:04:34.363 --> 00:04:36.483
So when you're comparing pricing across models,

123
00:04:36.603 --> 00:04:38.704
cheaper per token doesn't necessarily mean

124
00:04:38.804 --> 00:04:42.445
cheaper for the same job if one model uses more

125
00:04:42.505 --> 00:04:44.185
tokens to represent the same text.

126
00:04:45.065 --> 00:04:47.145
But tokens are the fundamental unit of cost,

127
00:04:47.625 --> 00:04:50.846
rate limiting, and context windows in the LLM

128
00:04:50.886 --> 00:04:51.166
world.

129
00:04:51.846 --> 00:04:53.366
So it's good to get comfortable with this

130
00:04:53.406 --> 00:04:55.607
concept because it comes up everywhere.

131
00:04:57.127 --> 00:05:00.028
So Luciano, given that all this stuff is

132
00:05:00.068 --> 00:05:01.888
everywhere now, what are we building?

133
00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.973
I guess, first of all, it's worth clarifying

134
00:05:05.053 --> 00:05:06.875
because I think at this point, almost everyone

135
00:05:06.935 --> 00:05:09.418
has used things like Cursor, Copilot,

136
00:05:09.478 --> 00:05:12.622
Claude Code, Codex, whatever, all these coding

137
00:05:12.682 --> 00:05:13.343
AI agents.

138
00:05:13.903 --> 00:05:15.866
And I think it's worth just saying that this is

139
00:05:15.906 --> 00:05:17.868
the technology that we are talking about.

140
00:05:18.128 --> 00:05:21.032
LLMs are effectively what's powering all of

141
00:05:21.052 --> 00:05:21.492
these tools.

142
00:05:22.233 --> 00:05:24.594
And that's one type of use cases.

143
00:05:24.614 --> 00:05:27.034
So these are like coding agents that you use

144
00:05:27.434 --> 00:05:29.295
whenever you're writing some code to get

145
00:05:29.775 --> 00:05:33.036
assistance from this intelligence and knowledge

146
00:05:33.076 --> 00:05:34.996
that has been baked into the model.

147
00:05:35.656 --> 00:05:38.157
But we see a lot of use cases where we

148
00:05:38.197 --> 00:05:41.317
are embedding LLMs directly into applications.

149
00:05:41.798 --> 00:05:43.978
Applications we build either for ourselves while

150
00:05:44.018 --> 00:05:46.139
we experiment and learn more about this

151
00:05:46.159 --> 00:05:48.539
technology, but we see an increasing demand from

152
00:05:48.619 --> 00:05:51.000
customers, and we've been already building a few

153
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:51.700
examples.

154
00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:53.781
And just to give you an idea,

155
00:05:53.981 --> 00:05:57.304
things we have been building are like smart data

156
00:05:57.344 --> 00:05:58.485
transformation pipelines.

157
00:05:58.605 --> 00:06:00.766
So you can imagine that there is an LLM

158
00:06:00.806 --> 00:06:03.689
component that helps the user to describe what

159
00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:04.990
they want in natural language.

160
00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.591
For example, I don't know, merge these two

161
00:06:07.671 --> 00:06:10.393
example datasets, normalize them in some kind of

162
00:06:10.414 --> 00:06:12.595
way, flag duplicates, for example.

163
00:06:13.156 --> 00:06:16.278
And then the system using the LLM is able to

164
00:06:16.358 --> 00:06:17.279
convert that

165
00:06:17.999 --> 00:06:20.280
natural language requirement into reproducible

166
00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:23.981
deterministic code that then can be baked into a

167
00:06:24.021 --> 00:06:26.501
pipeline that can be reused over time.

168
00:06:26.841 --> 00:06:29.282
So effectively, it's almost like giving somebody

169
00:06:29.302 --> 00:06:31.943
that doesn't necessarily know how to code that

170
00:06:32.003 --> 00:06:35.364
pipeline themselves an easier door to basically

171
00:06:35.404 --> 00:06:37.785
be able to describe what they want with a

172
00:06:37.825 --> 00:06:40.005
process that somehow gives them a preview of the

173
00:06:40.045 --> 00:06:42.506
results, slowly converge to something that is

174
00:06:42.586 --> 00:06:44.406
actually doing what they want to achieve,

175
00:06:44.546 --> 00:06:46.067
and then save that

176
00:06:46.727 --> 00:06:49.369
into reproducible code that can be reused later

177
00:06:49.429 --> 00:06:49.509
on.

178
00:06:49.929 --> 00:06:51.410
So imagine almost like a notebook,

179
00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.552
but rather than writing code, you use LLMs to

180
00:06:55.172 --> 00:06:56.993
get to the final version of the code that you

181
00:06:57.033 --> 00:06:57.534
want to use.

182
00:06:58.154 --> 00:06:59.355
And this is just one example.

183
00:06:59.415 --> 00:07:01.856
Another one that comes up a lot is for data

184
00:07:01.936 --> 00:07:02.517
analytics.

185
00:07:02.977 --> 00:07:05.038
Being able to generate queries, for example,

186
00:07:05.078 --> 00:07:07.640
for Athena, is one we have done recently.

187
00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.481
But you can extend that idea to other databases

188
00:07:10.541 --> 00:07:12.463
like Redshift, Postgres, Elasticsearch.

189
00:07:13.163 --> 00:07:15.104
And the idea is, again, if you don't know the

190
00:07:15.144 --> 00:07:17.304
specific language that is required to query the

191
00:07:17.324 --> 00:07:20.686
data, you can use an LLM to convert some kind

192
00:07:20.726 --> 00:07:23.307
of human language of that query.

193
00:07:23.627 --> 00:07:25.147
For example, I want to know, I don't know,

194
00:07:25.187 --> 00:07:27.268
the top 10 spenders in this e-commerce.

195
00:07:28.308 --> 00:07:29.869
Give me the username, for example.

196
00:07:30.609 --> 00:07:32.690
an LLM should be able to do a good job

197
00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.952
in converting that to a specific query for a

198
00:07:34.972 --> 00:07:37.933
database, and then you can execute that query

199
00:07:38.033 --> 00:07:39.454
and give the results to the user.

200
00:07:39.754 --> 00:07:42.116
So again, it's always about trying to lower the

201
00:07:42.176 --> 00:07:45.158
barrier of entry to use specific technology,

202
00:07:45.198 --> 00:07:48.179
so you can use more natural language and have

203
00:07:48.259 --> 00:07:51.141
the LLM do all the hard work of converting that

204
00:07:51.401 --> 00:07:53.703
language into something that's more specific

205
00:07:53.763 --> 00:07:56.524
for, I don't know, converting data in some way,

206
00:07:56.604 --> 00:07:57.305
making queries,

207
00:07:58.005 --> 00:07:59.145
But there are other use cases.

208
00:07:59.205 --> 00:08:01.166
For example, we can automatically generate

209
00:08:01.206 --> 00:08:04.928
dashboards, for example, based on some of these

210
00:08:05.408 --> 00:08:07.088
data pipelines that I mentioned before.

211
00:08:07.469 --> 00:08:10.090
Another example we built is the system is

212
00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:12.230
capable of understanding the type of data using

213
00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:15.692
an LLM and picking up some of the metrics that

214
00:08:15.812 --> 00:08:18.333
are more relevant and creating dashboards with

215
00:08:18.453 --> 00:08:21.334
charts that make possibly the most sense.

216
00:08:21.794 --> 00:08:23.255
And again, it's not always perfect.

217
00:08:23.315 --> 00:08:24.355
I think there is a lot of...

218
00:08:24.955 --> 00:08:27.176
DLM can generally do an average work and then

219
00:08:27.196 --> 00:08:29.917
you still want a human to maybe go in and

220
00:08:29.957 --> 00:08:30.377
refine.

221
00:08:30.798 --> 00:08:33.479
But we are seeing lots of use cases like these

222
00:08:33.619 --> 00:08:33.779
ones.

223
00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:36.040
And other ones we have seen online from...

224
00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:38.961
Just looking at other examples are,

225
00:08:38.981 --> 00:08:40.903
for example, customer support automation,

226
00:08:40.963 --> 00:08:43.945
where you can have a chatbot that helps the user

227
00:08:43.985 --> 00:08:47.047
to start asking questions and eventually route

228
00:08:47.087 --> 00:08:50.469
them maybe to an actual human agent that can

229
00:08:50.489 --> 00:08:52.550
help them to perform certain actions.

230
00:08:52.751 --> 00:08:55.352
Or sometimes it's the LLM itself that can do

231
00:08:55.412 --> 00:08:57.414
certain actions on behalf of the user.

232
00:08:58.034 --> 00:09:00.035
and other ones are document processing.

233
00:09:00.055 --> 00:09:03.076
So imagine like an OCR process, but much smarter

234
00:09:03.096 --> 00:09:05.557
than that, because a classic OCR will just give

235
00:09:05.577 --> 00:09:07.057
you the plain text.

236
00:09:07.697 --> 00:09:10.298
While when you combine OCR with an LLM,

237
00:09:10.318 --> 00:09:12.459
you can actually ask questions to documents and

238
00:09:12.499 --> 00:09:15.880
get out structured data from your documents.

239
00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.783
So, yeah, I guess before we get carried away

240
00:09:19.843 --> 00:09:21.984
with all these examples, I think it's fair to

241
00:09:22.104 --> 00:09:25.647
say, to remark at least, that these LLMs are not

242
00:09:25.707 --> 00:09:27.408
magic and they are not necessarily the right

243
00:09:27.428 --> 00:09:28.268
tool for everything.

244
00:09:29.109 --> 00:09:32.451
In general, I think LLMs are good to effectively

245
00:09:32.511 --> 00:09:35.353
convert this kind of requirement in some kind of

246
00:09:36.314 --> 00:09:38.375
human language, so you can type something and

247
00:09:38.415 --> 00:09:39.915
try to describe what you want to achieve,

248
00:09:40.575 --> 00:09:43.216
and train the LLM to convert that requirement

249
00:09:43.396 --> 00:09:46.497
into specific actions that will make sense for

250
00:09:46.517 --> 00:09:47.517
the system you're building.

251
00:09:47.537 --> 00:09:50.398
So basically, understanding text is one of the

252
00:09:50.438 --> 00:09:52.038
main superpowers of LLMs.

253
00:09:52.678 --> 00:09:55.159
But they are not good at everything that needs

254
00:09:55.199 --> 00:09:57.520
to be deterministic and precise, because they

255
00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:58.880
are probabilistic by nature.

256
00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:00.921
So if you ask the same question twice,

257
00:10:01.541 --> 00:10:03.801
probably you will get a slightly different

258
00:10:04.022 --> 00:10:05.082
answer the second time.

259
00:10:05.882 --> 00:10:08.283
Sometimes these LLMs can hallucinate,

260
00:10:08.464 --> 00:10:11.225
which is when they confidently state something

261
00:10:11.265 --> 00:10:13.507
that is not necessarily true, and they cannot

262
00:10:13.527 --> 00:10:15.888
necessarily do arithmetics very well.

263
00:10:16.088 --> 00:10:18.049
So sometimes they will do mistakes there.

264
00:10:18.369 --> 00:10:20.230
The classic example is if you tell,

265
00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.452
I don't know, how many vowels there are in the

266
00:10:23.472 --> 00:10:24.233
word strawberry,

267
00:10:24.833 --> 00:10:26.634
most of the time you might get a wrong response.

268
00:10:27.094 --> 00:10:29.375
This used to be one of the common jokes when

269
00:10:29.635 --> 00:10:30.555
LLMs came out.

270
00:10:30.855 --> 00:10:32.536
I think they are getting better, but the point

271
00:10:32.596 --> 00:10:34.877
is that there are lots of things that LLMs are

272
00:10:34.937 --> 00:10:37.418
good at and many things that they are bad at,

273
00:10:37.518 --> 00:10:39.259
so don't try to use them for everything.

274
00:10:39.299 --> 00:10:41.639
Try to understand what is good about them,

275
00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.441
what is bad, and then pick them only for the

276
00:10:44.481 --> 00:10:45.341
right use cases.

277
00:10:45.721 --> 00:10:47.823
And I think the key principle is try to use

278
00:10:47.943 --> 00:10:50.264
LLMs for everything that is a little bit fuzzy.

279
00:10:50.444 --> 00:10:52.526
Again, as a human that is trying to describe

280
00:10:52.606 --> 00:10:55.348
something, and you want to have that

281
00:10:55.428 --> 00:10:57.089
understanding of the description and do

282
00:10:57.429 --> 00:10:57.870
something.

283
00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.992
But all the precise parts, I think you should

284
00:11:01.032 --> 00:11:05.215
still try to use code and more regular

285
00:11:05.275 --> 00:11:07.556
automation to achieve those results in a more

286
00:11:07.596 --> 00:11:08.297
predictable way.

287
00:11:09.418 --> 00:11:11.159
I think, in general, the last point I want to

288
00:11:11.219 --> 00:11:13.021
make here is that it's going to become a

289
00:11:13.041 --> 00:11:15.703
standard building block, as many others that we

290
00:11:15.723 --> 00:11:16.984
have been using throughout the years.

291
00:11:17.765 --> 00:11:19.627
It's always, as any other building block,

292
00:11:19.687 --> 00:11:21.088
to understand what are the patterns,

293
00:11:21.488 --> 00:11:23.010
what are the good things, what are the bad

294
00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.531
things, what are the common problems,

295
00:11:24.611 --> 00:11:26.393
and hopefully, today, we are going to be able to

296
00:11:26.433 --> 00:11:27.834
cover some of that.

297
00:11:28.154 --> 00:11:30.736
Anything you want to add on this part,

298
00:11:30.796 --> 00:11:30.977
Eoin?

299
00:11:32.368 --> 00:11:36.209
Yeah, it's definitely, I think, recommended to

300
00:11:36.729 --> 00:11:38.570
experiment frequently and not assume,

301
00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:41.011
if you haven't already experimented frequently,

302
00:11:41.631 --> 00:11:46.232
that you just add a feature request and use LLMs

303
00:11:46.552 --> 00:11:48.453
in production for the first time, and it'll be

304
00:11:48.493 --> 00:11:49.053
smooth sailing.

305
00:11:49.553 --> 00:11:51.334
I think we've seen statistics that the vast,

306
00:11:51.414 --> 00:11:54.255
vast majority of these projects are not making

307
00:11:54.275 --> 00:11:55.195
it to production right now.

308
00:11:55.715 --> 00:11:56.755
for a whole host of reasons.

309
00:11:56.975 --> 00:11:58.316
Maybe people don't have the right data,

310
00:11:58.676 --> 00:12:00.156
they didn't have the right use case in mind,

311
00:12:00.956 --> 00:12:03.837
or it's the results just aren't effective enough

312
00:12:04.377 --> 00:12:07.358
to meet the use case that was envisaged.

313
00:12:07.998 --> 00:12:10.318
Like we find more success where the use case is

314
00:12:10.358 --> 00:12:12.399
very well defined and simple.

315
00:12:12.639 --> 00:12:13.679
And it's a good idea, I think,

316
00:12:13.739 --> 00:12:16.420
in general to focus on areas where you're

317
00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:18.820
spending a lot of time that you might benefit

318
00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:21.461
from this level of automation, but simple things

319
00:12:21.601 --> 00:12:24.002
rather than trying to assume that AI is so

320
00:12:24.042 --> 00:12:25.042
intelligent you can throw the

321
00:12:26.042 --> 00:12:27.524
the most complex problem you have at it,

322
00:12:27.564 --> 00:12:28.885
which usually ends in failure.

323
00:12:30.666 --> 00:12:32.928
We can maybe talk a bit about AI and Gen

324
00:12:32.968 --> 00:12:34.970
AI and what we mean by agents as well.

325
00:12:35.731 --> 00:12:38.893
AI has become shorthand for Gen AI in popular

326
00:12:38.933 --> 00:12:39.714
conversation, but

327
00:12:40.199 --> 00:12:42.639
Of course, AI is much broader and has a much

328
00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:43.580
longer history.

329
00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:46.420
Traditional machine learning classification,

330
00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:49.121
regression, anomaly detection is still AI and

331
00:12:49.161 --> 00:12:50.241
still incredibly useful.

332
00:12:50.721 --> 00:12:52.601
Computer vision, speech recognition,

333
00:12:53.242 --> 00:12:55.582
recommendation engines, they're all AI,

334
00:12:55.642 --> 00:12:57.362
but not necessarily Gen AI.

335
00:12:58.343 --> 00:13:00.743
And AWS has a whole ecosystem of services for

336
00:13:01.023 --> 00:13:03.104
the more traditional AI angle, like SageMaker,

337
00:13:03.504 --> 00:13:05.144
recognition, Textract, et cetera.

338
00:13:05.864 --> 00:13:07.086
We won't cover any of those today,

339
00:13:07.166 --> 00:13:09.028
but still worth knowing about, it doesn't always

340
00:13:09.068 --> 00:13:10.250
have to be LLM based.

341
00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:13.193
So Gen AI is specifically about generating new

342
00:13:13.253 --> 00:13:16.357
content like text, images, code, audio,

343
00:13:16.397 --> 00:13:16.837
and video.

344
00:13:17.578 --> 00:13:20.061
And when we say LLM inference in this episode,

345
00:13:20.101 --> 00:13:22.544
we're talking specifically about Gen AI.

346
00:13:23.665 --> 00:13:25.447
Now, inference in practice.

347
00:13:26.228 --> 00:13:27.830
is generally, at the simplest level,

348
00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:28.971
text generation.

349
00:13:28.991 --> 00:13:30.753
You send it a prompt, and then you get an

350
00:13:30.813 --> 00:13:32.194
answer, which we call a completion.

351
00:13:32.855 --> 00:13:35.197
But increasingly, we're also talking about

352
00:13:35.397 --> 00:13:37.479
agents and agentic workflows.

353
00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:40.743
And these are really, I suppose, more

354
00:13:40.783 --> 00:13:43.405
sophisticated loops where the LLM can

355
00:13:44.306 --> 00:13:46.267
try and reason or simulate reasoning,

356
00:13:46.367 --> 00:13:47.987
plan and take actions.

357
00:13:48.307 --> 00:13:50.648
It's like orchestration of multiple steps of an

358
00:13:50.768 --> 00:13:51.509
LLM, really.

359
00:13:52.169 --> 00:13:53.910
So what we mean when we talk about agents,

360
00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.170
like an agent is like a smart loop.

361
00:13:57.371 --> 00:13:59.612
Rather than hoping that you get a good

362
00:13:59.672 --> 00:14:01.852
completion back, the LLM receives a task,

363
00:14:01.932 --> 00:14:04.593
decides what to do, uses tools, observes the

364
00:14:04.633 --> 00:14:05.634
results and iterates.

365
00:14:06.948 --> 00:14:09.492
And this is one of the main things that makes

366
00:14:10.153 --> 00:14:14.138
agentic LLMs so powerful, the actual tools that

367
00:14:14.158 --> 00:14:14.619
you can use.

368
00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:17.283
With tools, you can expand the LLMs capabilities

369
00:14:17.363 --> 00:14:18.765
far beyond just generating text.

370
00:14:18.945 --> 00:14:21.809
We know that generating text alone is subject to

371
00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:27.874
hallucinations and errors, but by combining LLMs

372
00:14:28.094 --> 00:14:31.037
with access to deterministic tools,

373
00:14:31.137 --> 00:14:32.778
it can actually become very powerful.

374
00:14:33.238 --> 00:14:36.000
So the LLM itself still just generates text.

375
00:14:36.521 --> 00:14:38.743
It could describe what tool to call and with

376
00:14:38.783 --> 00:14:41.385
what parameters, but then your code executes the

377
00:14:41.465 --> 00:14:43.186
actual tool and feeds back the results.

378
00:14:43.847 --> 00:14:45.528
And you can write tools that do virtually

379
00:14:45.648 --> 00:14:47.429
anything, like check the weather at a given

380
00:14:47.469 --> 00:14:47.850
location,

381
00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:50.371
look up a customer record in a database,

382
00:14:50.811 --> 00:14:53.433
call a third-party API, read files,

383
00:14:53.493 --> 00:14:56.074
run code, or just trigger complex workflows on

384
00:14:56.114 --> 00:14:56.875
behalf of a user.

385
00:14:57.375 --> 00:14:59.796
And this is what turns an LLM from a fancy

386
00:14:59.856 --> 00:15:02.377
autocomplete into something that can actually

387
00:15:02.437 --> 00:15:03.878
take actions in the real world.

388
00:15:05.639 --> 00:15:07.440
With this power comes a lot of responsibility

389
00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.541
and safety boundaries are required that prevent

390
00:15:10.581 --> 00:15:12.042
the agent from doing things it shouldn't.

391
00:15:12.823 --> 00:15:15.764
So serious guardrails are required to stop it

392
00:15:15.804 --> 00:15:17.325
from executing destructive actions,

393
00:15:17.845 --> 00:15:20.966
leaking personal information, or going off

394
00:15:21.007 --> 00:15:22.347
topic.

395
00:15:23.148 --> 00:15:24.568
If you're doing it in an AWS world,

396
00:15:24.988 --> 00:15:28.850
defining roles and very minimal permissions help

397
00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.291
a lot with this, as well as your network

398
00:15:30.331 --> 00:15:30.711
boundaries.

399
00:15:32.552 --> 00:15:33.672
So when you have an agent as well,

400
00:15:33.732 --> 00:15:35.853
there's also consideration for memory and

401
00:15:35.913 --> 00:15:36.313
context.

402
00:15:36.793 --> 00:15:39.394
The agent will have to maintain state across

403
00:15:39.414 --> 00:15:42.015
steps, building up context as it works through a

404
00:15:42.055 --> 00:15:42.355
problem.

405
00:15:43.195 --> 00:15:46.896
So you can think of it as LLM plus tools,

406
00:15:47.196 --> 00:15:49.877
plus the loop, plus context management,

407
00:15:50.117 --> 00:15:50.797
plus guardrails.

408
00:15:51.377 --> 00:15:53.058
Put all those things together and you have an

409
00:15:53.118 --> 00:15:53.378
agent.

410
00:15:54.658 --> 00:15:58.380
In practice, there are loads of frameworks that

411
00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.261
help you build these patterns, like LangChain,

412
00:16:01.801 --> 00:16:05.282
strands from AWS, the Vercel AI SDK,

413
00:16:05.462 --> 00:16:07.723
and there's plenty more in every language.

414
00:16:08.564 --> 00:16:11.505
Plus, AWS has its own Bedrock Agents feature.

415
00:16:11.865 --> 00:16:13.526
We're not going to dive deep on the whole

416
00:16:13.546 --> 00:16:17.267
agentic side of things today, or on the AWS

417
00:16:17.647 --> 00:16:20.108
services specifically built to host and run

418
00:16:20.188 --> 00:16:21.109
agents at scale.

419
00:16:21.949 --> 00:16:23.710
That probably deserves its own episode,

420
00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:24.030
but

421
00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:26.971
It is important to understand what we generally

422
00:16:27.031 --> 00:16:29.472
mean by agentic because it shapes how you think

423
00:16:29.552 --> 00:16:30.332
about inference.

424
00:16:30.892 --> 00:16:33.153
It's not just one prompt in, one response out.

425
00:16:33.873 --> 00:16:37.194
Loops, tools are called, you've got context

426
00:16:37.234 --> 00:16:39.875
building, and it all translates to more tokens,

427
00:16:39.995 --> 00:16:42.415
more latency, and more things to think about

428
00:16:42.495 --> 00:16:44.136
when you're setting up your infrastructure.

429
00:16:44.376 --> 00:16:46.357
With all of this context in mind,

430
00:16:47.257 --> 00:16:49.438
let's talk about Bedrock, what it is,

431
00:16:49.978 --> 00:16:50.698
and why it exists.

432
00:16:51.078 --> 00:16:51.739
Yeah, exactly.

433
00:16:52.039 --> 00:16:55.403
So I think at a high level, Bedrock, we can think about

434
00:16:55.463 --> 00:16:58.287
it as the AWS managed service for accessing

435
00:16:58.347 --> 00:17:00.189
foundation models via API.

436
00:17:00.469 --> 00:17:03.152
So you can imagine it as a unified set of

437
00:17:03.212 --> 00:17:05.835
APIs for calling hundreds of models

438
00:17:06.636 --> 00:17:09.137
And there are many providers, like we mentioned

439
00:17:09.177 --> 00:17:11.277
already, some of them Amazon, Anthropic,

440
00:17:11.337 --> 00:17:13.578
Meta, Mistral.ai, DeepSeek, OpenAI,

441
00:17:13.618 --> 00:17:13.998
and more.

442
00:17:14.398 --> 00:17:19.080
And through this unified API, you basically get

443
00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:20.160
a few interesting things.

444
00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:22.080
The first one is that you don't need separate

445
00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:25.161
accounts or API keys for each provider because

446
00:17:25.341 --> 00:17:27.222
Bedrock is kind of your own central place.

447
00:17:28.242 --> 00:17:30.505
And it runs within the AWS ecosystem,

448
00:17:30.565 --> 00:17:32.567
which means that you can also use IAM for

