00:17:55.708 --> 00:17:59.130
providers directly, OpenAI as its own API,

462
00:17:59.251 --> 00:18:00.611
Anthropic as its own API.

463
00:18:01.092 --> 00:18:03.253
Pretty much every provider needs to give you

464
00:18:03.313 --> 00:18:06.436
access to the model when they offer the cloud

465
00:18:06.476 --> 00:18:08.457
version of the model through an API.

466
00:18:08.497 --> 00:18:09.978
So you can just go through them,

467
00:18:10.118 --> 00:18:12.460
create an account, and call their API directly.

468
00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:14.381
And I think this is not too bad,

469
00:18:14.441 --> 00:18:15.482
it probably works fine.

470
00:18:15.762 --> 00:18:17.864
If you're doing prototyping and small projects,

471
00:18:17.944 --> 00:18:19.965
it might actually be a little bit simpler than

472
00:18:20.025 --> 00:18:22.067
just getting started with Bedrock,

473
00:18:22.187 --> 00:18:23.928
which probably comes with a little bit of extra

474
00:18:23.948 --> 00:18:26.250
complexity, especially if you're not too

475
00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:27.510
familiar with AWS.

476
00:18:27.811 --> 00:18:29.772
But I think then you need to know what you're

477
00:18:29.812 --> 00:18:31.834
missing out, because I think you need to

478
00:18:31.854 --> 00:18:33.455
understand that if you want to go

479
00:18:33.515 --> 00:18:35.676
production-ready, probably what Bedrock is

480
00:18:35.716 --> 00:18:37.898
giving you is worth it, and it's worth the

481
00:18:38.058 --> 00:18:40.600
initial effort of learning Bedrock and learning

482
00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:42.241
all the tools that you get with Bedrock.

483
00:18:42.601 --> 00:18:43.922
And just to give you a few examples,

484
00:18:44.803 --> 00:18:46.764
you will get security and compliance because

485
00:18:46.864 --> 00:18:49.426
basically this is probably one of the main

486
00:18:49.486 --> 00:18:51.207
selling points, especially if you're working in

487
00:18:51.267 --> 00:18:55.411
industries where it is important to respect the

488
00:18:55.691 --> 00:18:57.112
privacy of the data of users.

489
00:18:59.033 --> 00:19:01.876
what Bedrock guarantees you is that data stays

490
00:19:01.976 --> 00:19:03.718
within your AWS account boundary.

491
00:19:03.838 --> 00:19:06.941
You can pick specific regions where the

492
00:19:07.041 --> 00:19:07.882
inference runs.

493
00:19:08.022 --> 00:19:10.885
So if you have also legal requirements where you

494
00:19:10.925 --> 00:19:13.267
need to make sure that your data never leaves a

495
00:19:13.307 --> 00:19:15.450
specific region, like Europe, for example,

496
00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:17.251
you can do that through Bedrock.

497
00:19:17.992 --> 00:19:20.956
data is encrypted in transit and at rest.

498
00:19:22.758 --> 00:19:24.920
Effectively, there is an agreement between AWS

499
00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:27.023
and the model provider that they will never use

500
00:19:27.443 --> 00:19:29.205
data that you send to the models to do

501
00:19:29.325 --> 00:19:30.627
additional training in the future.

502
00:19:30.647 --> 00:19:32.469
This is probably one of the biggest selling

503
00:19:32.509 --> 00:19:33.330
points for Bedrock.

504
00:19:34.191 --> 00:19:37.032
So effectively, you can trust Bedrock a little

505
00:19:37.092 --> 00:19:39.813
bit more than just having to go through the

506
00:19:39.873 --> 00:19:41.533
agreements that you will get with each

507
00:19:41.673 --> 00:19:43.714
individual provider, which is probably going to

508
00:19:43.734 --> 00:19:45.475
be very different terms and conditions.

509
00:19:45.495 --> 00:19:47.175
So if you want to test, for example,

510
00:19:47.775 --> 00:19:50.816
both Anthropic and OpenAI models, you probably

511
00:19:50.836 --> 00:19:52.517
need to go and read through the two different

512
00:19:52.557 --> 00:19:54.298
agreements and understand if they will work out

513
00:19:54.338 --> 00:19:54.618
for you.

514
00:19:55.018 --> 00:19:56.758
While with Bedrock, you have a more unified

515
00:19:56.798 --> 00:19:57.278
experience.

516
00:19:57.399 --> 00:19:58.679
Once you understand the guarantees,

517
00:19:59.199 --> 00:20:01.000
you have a system that allows you to try

518
00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:01.760
different models.

519
00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:03.621
Then we already mentioned governance,

520
00:20:03.701 --> 00:20:05.682
because you can use IAM and CloudTrail.

521
00:20:06.622 --> 00:20:08.443
You have, again, model flexibility.

522
00:20:08.563 --> 00:20:10.664
I already mentioned that, where if you want to

523
00:20:10.765 --> 00:20:12.986
try to see which model works best for you,

524
00:20:13.646 --> 00:20:15.687
once you are in Bedrock, it's relatively easy to

525
00:20:15.727 --> 00:20:17.848
switch between the models that are available

526
00:20:17.888 --> 00:20:18.108
there.

527
00:20:18.529 --> 00:20:21.790
And then there are a bunch of interesting

528
00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:24.091
Bedrock-specific features, which I don't think

529
00:20:24.111 --> 00:20:25.772
we're going to be spending a lot of time on

530
00:20:26.112 --> 00:20:28.694
them today, but you can easily build a knowledge

531
00:20:28.754 --> 00:20:30.355
base, sometimes called RAG,

532
00:20:31.095 --> 00:20:33.377
We already mentioned agents, there are entire

533
00:20:33.517 --> 00:20:36.999
subservices within Bedrock and frameworks that

534
00:20:37.039 --> 00:20:39.781
allow you to make it easy to build and run

535
00:20:39.942 --> 00:20:40.962
agents in production.

536
00:20:41.383 --> 00:20:42.944
You have the concept of guardrails,

537
00:20:43.044 --> 00:20:46.627
so being able to effectively limit some of the

538
00:20:46.667 --> 00:20:48.668
capabilities of an LLM, for example,

539
00:20:48.708 --> 00:20:51.270
if you want to make sure it doesn't go off

540
00:20:51.590 --> 00:20:52.951
on a path that you don't like,

541
00:20:53.031 --> 00:20:53.592
maybe, I don't know.

542
00:20:54.052 --> 00:20:57.397
Classic examples are like limiting the LLM

543
00:20:57.417 --> 00:20:59.800
interaction to, for example, not be able to talk

544
00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:04.947
about politics or maybe not go outside a scope

545
00:21:05.107 --> 00:21:07.010
that maybe is the scope that is specific in the

546
00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.033
domain where you are implementing the LLM.

547
00:21:09.393 --> 00:21:12.995
Or maybe you can remove some PII,

548
00:21:13.015 --> 00:21:16.496
so there are ways to detect that PII is coming

549
00:21:16.556 --> 00:21:18.097
into the conversation with the LLM,

550
00:21:18.137 --> 00:21:20.898
so you could obfuscate some of that PII before

551
00:21:21.359 --> 00:21:22.479
it goes back to the user.

552
00:21:22.799 --> 00:21:24.820
So you have all these additional features that I

553
00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:27.562
think are really important for when you're about

554
00:21:27.582 --> 00:21:29.422
to go to production and you want to make sure

555
00:21:29.803 --> 00:21:30.563
you are ready for it.

556
00:21:30.883 --> 00:21:34.544
Now, there is one interesting caveat that I find

557
00:21:34.884 --> 00:21:36.464
a little bit disappointing sometimes,

558
00:21:37.125 --> 00:21:42.006
because although I said that there is support

559
00:21:42.026 --> 00:21:44.887
for hundreds of models, not all the mainstream

560
00:21:44.947 --> 00:21:45.947
models are out there.

561
00:21:46.007 --> 00:21:48.107
For example, a good example is Gemini,

562
00:21:48.167 --> 00:21:50.568
which is a very capable model, and it's not

563
00:21:50.648 --> 00:21:52.008
currently available in Bedrock.

564
00:21:52.088 --> 00:21:53.829
You can imagine this is due to

565
00:21:54.589 --> 00:21:57.334
competitive reasons because Gemini being from

566
00:21:57.374 --> 00:22:00.699
Google, of course, it's sold through Google

567
00:22:00.739 --> 00:22:01.100
Cloud.

568
00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:04.385
So I don't think it's very easy for AWS and

569
00:22:04.485 --> 00:22:06.689
Google to agree on a way to make that work

570
00:22:07.009 --> 00:22:08.271
on AWS as well.

571
00:22:08.959 --> 00:22:11.901
I know that it's currently the same for OpenAI.

572
00:22:11.921 --> 00:22:15.624
There are the GPT-oss models available,

573
00:22:16.045 --> 00:22:17.246
but you don't get GPT.

574
00:22:17.326 --> 00:22:21.009
For example, 5.3 will be the bleeding edge model

575
00:22:21.049 --> 00:22:21.569
at the moment.

576
00:22:22.089 --> 00:22:23.891
That's not currently available in Bedrock.

577
00:22:24.451 --> 00:22:26.513
I suspect that that might change because I'm

578
00:22:26.533 --> 00:22:28.675
hearing that there is a big round of investment

579
00:22:28.815 --> 00:22:29.715
coming into OpenAI.

580
00:22:30.636 --> 00:22:32.157
where Amazon is taking part.

581
00:22:32.177 --> 00:22:34.019
So maybe that will change soon enough.

582
00:22:34.219 --> 00:22:36.360
They announced as part of that, that the GPT

583
00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:38.242
models would become available in Bedrock.

584
00:22:38.602 --> 00:22:40.103
So that is the plan.

585
00:22:40.604 --> 00:22:41.224
Yeah.

586
00:22:41.284 --> 00:22:42.385
It only cost $50 billion.

587
00:22:42.425 --> 00:22:43.886
That was the price.

588
00:22:43.966 --> 00:22:44.467
Exactly.

589
00:22:45.007 --> 00:22:47.089
So yeah, right now, just be aware that if you

590
00:22:47.109 --> 00:22:50.031
want to use Gemini currently, that's not going

591
00:22:50.051 --> 00:22:51.051
to be available.

592
00:22:51.132 --> 00:22:52.973
It's probably not going to be available for a

593
00:22:53.013 --> 00:22:53.413
long time.

594
00:22:53.794 --> 00:22:55.095
While if you're interested in GPT,

595
00:22:55.875 --> 00:22:58.237
Models, they will probably become available very

596
00:22:58.277 --> 00:23:00.759
soon, but not just right now, the moment

597
00:23:01.099 --> 00:23:01.299
we are

598
00:23:01.319 --> 00:23:02.000
recording this.

599
00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:04.362
And again, just want to remark that there is so

600
00:23:04.422 --> 00:23:06.163
much to talk about when it comes to Bedrock.

601
00:23:06.343 --> 00:23:08.385
Today, we're going to focus just on trying to

602
00:23:08.465 --> 00:23:10.326
use the LLM programmatically part.

603
00:23:10.867 --> 00:23:12.988
Maybe we'll have future episodes if there is

604
00:23:13.128 --> 00:23:15.610
enough interest, and if we get to learn enough

605
00:23:17.092 --> 00:23:18.933
to make it work for us to create an entire

606
00:23:18.973 --> 00:23:20.654
episode dedicated to the other features.

607
00:23:21.235 --> 00:23:23.837
So with all of that introduction, how do we get

608
00:23:23.897 --> 00:23:25.178
started using Bedrock?

609
00:23:26.872 --> 00:23:30.133
We talked about Bedrock maybe well over a year

610
00:23:30.173 --> 00:23:30.714
ago, I think.

611
00:23:31.254 --> 00:23:33.575
And since then, there's a new access model.

612
00:23:33.795 --> 00:23:35.236
So the first thing you need to do is understand

613
00:23:35.276 --> 00:23:35.896
this a little bit.

614
00:23:36.576 --> 00:23:39.458
You'll find a lot of outdated articles out

615
00:23:39.498 --> 00:23:39.698
there.

616
00:23:39.798 --> 00:23:42.299
Bedrock used to have a model access page where

617
00:23:42.339 --> 00:23:44.060
you had to manually enable each model.

618
00:23:45.260 --> 00:23:47.242
In commercial regions, that old workflow is

619
00:23:47.262 --> 00:23:47.663
gone.

620
00:23:47.963 --> 00:23:51.466
Today, access is mostly IAM plus one-time

621
00:23:51.506 --> 00:23:52.627
agreements for some models.

622
00:23:53.227 --> 00:23:54.368
So you'll want to follow the current

623
00:23:54.388 --> 00:23:56.991
documentation rather than anything from 2023.

624
00:23:58.512 --> 00:24:00.554
Models are now available by default in

625
00:24:00.574 --> 00:24:02.776
commercial regions as long as your IAM identity

626
00:24:03.096 --> 00:24:05.578
has the right permissions, like bedrock:InvokeModel.

627
00:24:06.198 --> 00:24:08.861
This brings Bedrock in line with how other AWS

628
00:24:08.961 --> 00:24:10.342
services work, which is nice.

629
00:24:11.583 --> 00:24:12.944
There are a couple of things that can trip you

630
00:24:13.025 --> 00:24:13.585
up, right?

631
00:24:13.665 --> 00:24:16.788
So some Bedrock serverless models are served

632
00:24:16.868 --> 00:24:18.590
from the AWS Marketplace.

633
00:24:19.131 --> 00:24:20.712
The first time your account uses one of those,

634
00:24:21.273 --> 00:24:22.954
Bedrock automatically tries to create a

635
00:24:23.114 --> 00:24:25.997
Marketplace subscription, and you need IAM

636
00:24:26.017 --> 00:24:29.240
permissions for AWS Marketplace in case that's

637
00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:30.161
something that trips you up.

638
00:24:31.082 --> 00:24:33.244
Note that models from Amazon, DeepSeek,

639
00:24:33.304 --> 00:24:37.528
Mistral, Meta, Quen, and OpenAI are not sold

640
00:24:37.648 --> 00:24:38.609
through the marketplace.

641
00:24:38.689 --> 00:24:40.511
So this only applies to certain providers.

642
00:24:41.111 --> 00:24:43.453
We'll talk more about this gotcha in a little

643
00:24:43.493 --> 00:24:43.734
while.

644
00:24:44.695 --> 00:24:47.157
Anthropic models specifically still require a

645
00:24:47.237 --> 00:24:49.179
one-time use case submission.

646
00:24:49.619 --> 00:24:50.760
You just fill out a little form.

647
00:24:52.141 --> 00:24:53.683
and you can invoke them.

648
00:24:53.843 --> 00:24:55.565
You can complete this through the Bedrock

649
00:24:55.605 --> 00:24:57.647
Playground in the console or using the API.

650
00:24:58.408 --> 00:25:00.890
And if you use AWS Organizations, if you

651
00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:03.493
complete it at the management account level via

652
00:25:03.573 --> 00:25:06.176
API, it extends the approval to all organization

653
00:25:06.216 --> 00:25:06.456
accounts.

654
00:25:07.757 --> 00:25:09.517
Once that bit is done, you can pick a model.

655
00:25:10.057 --> 00:25:12.158
And you've got the Claude models, as we

656
00:25:12.198 --> 00:25:13.838
mentioned, which are very popular,

657
00:25:13.918 --> 00:25:16.599
excellent for decent complex reasoning,

658
00:25:16.799 --> 00:25:18.099
coding, long documents.

659
00:25:19.199 --> 00:25:20.619
This is one of the ones we use the most,

660
00:25:20.679 --> 00:25:20.919
I think.

661
00:25:21.039 --> 00:25:22.960
And then you've got the Amazon's own Nova ones,

662
00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:25.800
which are the kind of the budget option,

663
00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:27.641
you know, good for price performance,

664
00:25:29.021 --> 00:25:30.941
balancing, especially for simpler tasks.

665
00:25:31.561 --> 00:25:34.702
And the light and micro Nova ones are very cost

666
00:25:34.762 --> 00:25:35.102
effective.

667
00:25:35.942 --> 00:25:38.266
You have the Meta Llama ones, open weight

668
00:25:38.286 --> 00:25:40.729
models, good general purpose.

669
00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:43.854
Maybe starting to show its age, I don't see a

670
00:25:43.894 --> 00:25:44.776
lot of use of them.

671
00:25:45.397 --> 00:25:48.962
And then Mistral is good for coding and

672
00:25:49.222 --> 00:25:50.544
multilingual tasks.

673
00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:56.561
Qwen and GLM are really starting to make an

674
00:25:56.621 --> 00:25:57.181
impact, I think.

675
00:25:57.201 --> 00:25:58.721
And in our opinion, there's lots of potential

676
00:25:58.881 --> 00:26:00.822
for those to be competitive in price.

677
00:26:01.202 --> 00:26:02.142
So that's just some examples.

678
00:26:02.622 --> 00:26:05.003
And as you mentioned, Luciano, you don't have

679
00:26:05.103 --> 00:26:08.023
all of the competitor models, but we can expect

680
00:26:08.143 --> 00:26:12.724
OpenAI's ones to become available at some point

681
00:26:12.804 --> 00:26:15.585
in the future, provided that agreement goes

682
00:26:15.645 --> 00:26:15.825
well.

683
00:26:17.866 --> 00:26:19.608
Good idea to start with a capable model like

684
00:26:19.648 --> 00:26:21.290
Cloud Sonnet to validate your approach,

685
00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:24.273
then see if a cheaper, faster model can handle

686
00:26:24.313 --> 00:26:24.373
it.

687
00:26:24.794 --> 00:26:26.456
No point in prematurely optimizing.

688
00:26:27.297 --> 00:26:29.739
And the Bedrock web console offers a good UI

689
00:26:29.759 --> 00:26:31.421
that allows you to send messages to multiple

690
00:26:31.561 --> 00:26:33.684
LLMs at the same time, so you can compare

691
00:26:33.704 --> 00:26:34.204
responses.

692
00:26:35.666 --> 00:26:37.767
Probably worth also mentioning that,

693
00:26:38.467 --> 00:26:40.728
as you can imagine, everybody's experimenting

694
00:26:41.109 --> 00:26:42.710
with Bedrock and with LLMs.

695
00:26:43.310 --> 00:26:46.232
And as a result of that, it might be more

696
00:26:46.272 --> 00:26:48.133
difficult than you expect to get the quotas you

697
00:26:48.153 --> 00:26:49.754
might need if you really start to run this at

698
00:26:49.834 --> 00:26:51.054
production and need the scale.

699
00:26:51.475 --> 00:26:55.477
So prepare to have to make a business case and

700
00:26:55.977 --> 00:27:00.420
plead for quotas that are beyond prototype POC

701
00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:00.660
scale.

702
00:27:02.389 --> 00:27:04.570
Once you have your model, you can call the API.

703
00:27:04.971 --> 00:27:07.092
So we're talking about the Invoke model or the

704
00:27:07.332 --> 00:27:10.534
Converse API for the more standard chat

705
00:27:10.554 --> 00:27:10.954
interface.

706
00:27:11.654 --> 00:27:13.815
We generally recommend using the Converse API.

707
00:27:14.296 --> 00:27:16.857
It's more of a unified interface across models

708
00:27:16.997 --> 00:27:19.879
with a consistent format, a bit like OpenAI's

709
00:27:20.079 --> 00:27:20.819
chat completions.

710
00:27:21.980 --> 00:27:23.701
And it's not all about text as well.

711
00:27:23.741 --> 00:27:26.683
You can use images and documents in the Converse

712
00:27:26.743 --> 00:27:28.684
API in the same message format.

713
00:27:28.764 --> 00:27:31.965
So multimodal use cases work out of the box.

714
00:27:32.305 --> 00:27:35.067
And it supports streaming with Converse Stream

715
00:27:35.247 --> 00:27:37.148
for real-time token-by-token output,

716
00:27:37.528 --> 00:27:40.149
which, if you're doing chat, is probably a

717
00:27:40.209 --> 00:27:40.650
must-have.

718
00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:44.411
And you have the AWS SDK for doing this in

719
00:27:44.431 --> 00:27:46.751
your language of choice, Python, Boto3,

720
00:27:47.492 --> 00:27:49.412
JavaScript, TypeScript, Java, et cetera.

721
00:27:51.873 --> 00:27:55.353
These SDKs are generally split into two parts,

722
00:27:55.534 --> 00:27:57.854
like one for the control plane and one for the

723
00:27:57.894 --> 00:27:58.314
runtime.

724
00:27:58.654 --> 00:28:00.775
So if you look at the Boto3 option,

725
00:28:00.975 --> 00:28:03.235
Bedrock Runtime is probably the one you'll be

726
00:28:03.335 --> 00:28:04.336
using more often.

727
00:28:04.856 --> 00:28:07.857
And the Bedrock one is just for control plane

728
00:28:07.897 --> 00:28:09.237
stuff, management of.

729
00:28:09.977 --> 00:28:11.319
Bedrock models, that kind of thing.

730
00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:14.582
A new thing as well, since the last one we

731
00:28:14.642 --> 00:28:16.804
talked about Bedrock is cross region inference.

732
00:28:18.385 --> 00:28:20.848
This lets AWS route your request to whichever

733
00:28:20.968 --> 00:28:23.530
region has availability and capacity.

734
00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:26.093
And it's a pretty big deal because I think this

735
00:28:26.153 --> 00:28:28.014
is the first time, there used to be like a

736
00:28:28.815 --> 00:28:31.057
adage that you could say, if you wanted to do

737
00:28:31.097 --> 00:28:33.620
something in multi-region in AWS, you had to

738
00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:35.702
specifically configure it in each region and

739
00:28:35.762 --> 00:28:37.083
configure the synchronization.

740
00:28:37.563 --> 00:28:39.365
This is the first time where you've got pretty

741
00:28:39.425 --> 00:28:41.547
much seamless routing from one region to

742
00:28:41.607 --> 00:28:41.847
another.

743
00:28:42.568 --> 00:28:46.432
And we can imagine that this is just down to

744
00:28:46.452 --> 00:28:48.874
the fact that GPU availability is scarce,

745
00:28:49.054 --> 00:28:51.356
so it makes sense to distribute it to whatever

746
00:28:51.396 --> 00:28:52.998
region has capacity.

747
00:28:54.574 --> 00:28:57.175
And the way you do that is by using a

748
00:28:58.355 --> 00:28:58.935
model ID.

749
00:28:59.595 --> 00:29:01.396
You've got a model ID, which might be something

750
00:29:01.436 --> 00:29:04.897
like anthropic.cloudsonnet4-version1.

751
00:29:05.637 --> 00:29:08.478
But a cross-region inference profile ID is

752
00:29:08.498 --> 00:29:09.798
something you can use instead.

753
00:29:09.978 --> 00:29:12.219
And it will have a routing prefix,

754
00:29:12.239 --> 00:29:12.779
like us.

755
00:29:12.939 --> 00:29:14.079
or eu.

756
00:29:14.239 --> 00:29:16.500
Or it could be a global routing,

757
00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:17.220
like global.

758
00:29:19.147 --> 00:29:20.368
That'll give you maximum throughput,

759
00:29:20.408 --> 00:29:21.788
but no geographic restriction.

760
00:29:21.928 --> 00:29:23.609
So it depends on your compliance, data

761
00:29:23.629 --> 00:29:25.830
retention, data residency requirements.

762
00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:28.651
New prefixes and profiles might be added over

763
00:29:28.691 --> 00:29:28.891
time.

764
00:29:30.252 --> 00:29:32.193
And at the SDK level, they're pretty much

765
00:29:32.233 --> 00:29:33.353
interchangeable.

766
00:29:33.373 --> 00:29:35.974
I think if you look in our,

767
00:29:36.694 --> 00:29:38.495
I think it was in our PodWhisperer where we use

768
00:29:38.895 --> 00:29:40.736
Bedrock, we talked about that in recent episodes

769
00:29:40.856 --> 00:29:42.397
that you could see in the commit history when we

770
00:29:42.437 --> 00:29:44.638
started using these inference profiles.

771
00:29:46.199 --> 00:29:49.661
Newer models like certain Claude and Lama

772
00:29:49.681 --> 00:29:51.702
versions only work through an inference profile.

773
00:29:51.803 --> 00:29:53.604
That's why we had to change it in ours,

774
00:29:53.884 --> 00:29:56.065
and we'll return a validation exception with

775
00:29:56.466 --> 00:29:58.127
on-demand throughput isn't supported.

776
00:29:58.667 --> 00:29:59.968
I think we were a bit confused when we saw

777
00:30:00.008 --> 00:30:00.729
that for the first time.

778
00:30:02.390 --> 00:30:03.631
If you hit this error, just add the routing

779
00:30:03.651 --> 00:30:04.832
prefix to your model ID.

780
00:30:05.192 --> 00:30:07.213
And the IAM permissions are different as well,

781
00:30:07.253 --> 00:30:08.594
so you'll have to make sure you set that up.

782
00:30:10.876 --> 00:30:12.516
If you're thinking about observability and

783
00:30:12.556 --> 00:30:14.757
monitoring these, you'll want to know where your

784
00:30:14.797 --> 00:30:15.818
requests got routed to.

785
00:30:15.838 --> 00:30:18.599
So you might check CloudTrail.

786
00:30:19.119 --> 00:30:21.640
Rerouted requests include an inference region

787
00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:21.940
field.

788
00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:26.462
And you can set up a CloudWatch metric filter on

789
00:30:26.502 --> 00:30:28.663
this to monitor your routing patterns.

790
00:30:29.103 --> 00:30:31.484
So I guess by default, if you're not too

791
00:30:31.524 --> 00:30:33.725
concerned, just default to using inference

792
00:30:33.765 --> 00:30:35.846
profile IDs, but for everything, there isn't

793
00:30:35.886 --> 00:30:36.827
really much of a downside.

794
00:30:37.207 --> 00:30:39.508
You just might want to think about the region

795
00:30:39.528 --> 00:30:41.069
you want to use and where you want your data

796
00:30:41.269 --> 00:30:41.569
to go.

797
00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:44.551
So given that, you're up and running,

798
00:30:44.691 --> 00:30:45.511
you can do inference.

799
00:30:46.371 --> 00:30:48.613
Should we talk about the cost and see if we

800
00:30:48.633 --> 00:30:51.294
can make it clear how much it might cost?

801
00:30:51.794 --> 00:30:54.715
Yes, it's actually not that difficult in terms

802
00:30:54.815 --> 00:30:57.715
of just the arithmetics of it, because we talked

803
00:30:57.775 --> 00:30:58.656
already about tokens.

804
00:30:58.936 --> 00:31:01.556
Tokens are generally classified in tokens in and

805
00:31:01.596 --> 00:31:03.257
tokens out, or input and output.

806
00:31:03.837 --> 00:31:06.097
Input is what the prompt you send to the LLM,

807
00:31:06.357 --> 00:31:09.198
output is the completion that gets generated by

808
00:31:09.238 --> 00:31:09.738
the LLM.

809
00:31:10.198 --> 00:31:13.479
And interesting enough, those get different

810
00:31:13.499 --> 00:31:17.480
prices, like a price for input, sometimes as

811
00:31:18.220 --> 00:31:20.122
million of tokens, sometimes in the thousand.

812
00:31:20.202 --> 00:31:22.844
I think it was actually changed recently that

813
00:31:22.904 --> 00:31:25.847
now in the pricing pages by the thousand used to

814
00:31:25.887 --> 00:31:28.769
be by millions, which confused me when we were

815
00:31:29.209 --> 00:31:31.251
writing the notes for this episode.

816
00:31:32.012 --> 00:31:33.773
But yeah, it doesn't change at the end of the

817
00:31:33.793 --> 00:31:33.873
day.

818
00:31:33.893 --> 00:31:36.075
The actual pricing is just a way of visualizing

819
00:31:36.115 --> 00:31:38.818
the cost unit for input and output.

820
00:31:38.898 --> 00:31:40.779
And each model is different, so make sure to

821
00:31:40.839 --> 00:31:41.060
check

822
00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:44.361
what is the cost for the specific model you want

823
00:31:44.381 --> 00:31:44.721
to use.

824
00:31:45.362 --> 00:31:47.843
Some models are more expensive than others.

825
00:31:48.603 --> 00:31:50.804
Generally, the bigger, more capable models are

826
00:31:50.864 --> 00:31:52.825
more expensive, but those are generally the ones

827
00:31:52.845 --> 00:31:54.445
that can be more reliable if you're doing

828
00:31:54.505 --> 00:31:55.386
complex tasks.

829
00:31:55.526 --> 00:31:58.327
So again, it might be worth starting with the

830
00:31:58.467 --> 00:32:00.728
more advanced ones just to make sure you can

831
00:32:00.868 --> 00:32:03.449
refine the first implementation of what you want

832
00:32:03.469 --> 00:32:05.450
to try to achieve, refine your prompt and

833
00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:05.830
everything.

834
00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:06.970
When you have something that works,

835
00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:10.011
you can try to see if cheaper models can also

836
00:32:10.051 --> 00:32:10.852
handle that task.

837
00:32:11.592 --> 00:32:13.594
as reliably as the more expensive model.

838
00:32:13.934 --> 00:32:16.196
And that's just a strategy to reduce cost.

839
00:32:16.917 --> 00:32:19.579
The interesting thing is that there is no

840
00:32:19.599 --> 00:32:20.400
upfront commitment.

841
00:32:20.420 --> 00:32:23.423
So as many other AWS services, you just pay for

842
00:32:23.463 --> 00:32:25.925
what you use, which is nice because if you have

843
00:32:25.985 --> 00:32:28.827
very occasional use cases, or maybe you don't

844
00:32:28.888 --> 00:32:30.649
know exactly how much you're going to be using

845
00:32:30.689 --> 00:32:32.831
an LLM power feature, that gives you an

846
00:32:32.851 --> 00:32:34.693
opportunity to grow as you go.

847
00:32:35.173 --> 00:32:37.354
There are a couple of tricks that you can use

848
00:32:37.414 --> 00:32:40.256
to reduce costs if you have specific use cases.

849
00:32:40.516 --> 00:32:42.037
One of these is batch inference.

850
00:32:42.417 --> 00:32:44.719
I honestly haven't tried it yet, but my

851
00:32:44.759 --> 00:32:47.280
understanding is that basically you can defer

852
00:32:47.300 --> 00:32:50.962
the execution of a bunch of LLM requests.

853
00:32:51.563 --> 00:32:53.804
So you have a little bit of a higher latency

854
00:32:53.864 --> 00:32:55.305
in being able to get the response,

855
00:32:55.785 --> 00:32:58.847
but that comes with a 50% discount on the cost

856
00:32:58.927 --> 00:33:00.248
of input and output tokens.

857
00:33:01.248 --> 00:33:04.052
If you don't have a real-time type of experience

858
00:33:04.072 --> 00:33:06.335
where a user is waiting for a response in line,

859
00:33:06.395 --> 00:33:08.118
maybe you're doing some kind of, I don't know,

860
00:33:08.218 --> 00:33:10.801
overnight batch processing, and you need to do,

861
00:33:11.182 --> 00:33:13.425
maybe analyze lots of documents, whatever it is,

862
00:33:14.006 --> 00:33:16.149
probably you can use batch inference to bring

863
00:33:16.229 --> 00:33:17.911
the cost significantly down.

864
00:33:19.032 --> 00:33:21.873
And then there are service tiers, which also is

865
00:33:21.913 --> 00:33:24.554
not something I have really invested a lot of

866
00:33:24.614 --> 00:33:26.775
time into really experimenting with,

867
00:33:26.935 --> 00:33:29.497
but effectively, there are different tiers of

868
00:33:29.897 --> 00:33:34.339
discounts and costs that you can use to try to

869
00:33:34.359 --> 00:33:36.199
bring your cost down, or maybe you can have one

870
00:33:36.239 --> 00:33:38.180
or three months commitments with reserved,

871
00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:41.041
and that will give you more guaranteed capacity

872
00:33:41.121 --> 00:33:42.442
for predictable workloads.

873
00:33:42.902 --> 00:33:45.284
So just make sure also to check the service tier

874
00:33:45.745 --> 00:33:48.627
in the pricing page to understand what that's

875
00:33:48.727 --> 00:33:50.729
all about, because it could be important for

876
00:33:50.769 --> 00:33:51.409
your use case.

877
00:33:51.910 --> 00:33:54.252
Then there are a few other things that can be

878
00:33:54.632 --> 00:33:55.333
relevant here.

879
00:33:55.413 --> 00:33:57.254
For example, there is a concept of prompt

880
00:33:57.354 --> 00:33:59.957
caching, which basically allows you to

881
00:34:01.758 --> 00:34:04.902
reduce the amount of tokens that get sent to the

882
00:34:04.962 --> 00:34:05.303
LLM.

883
00:34:05.443 --> 00:34:09.128
So that's another way that can save money on the

884
00:34:09.248 --> 00:34:10.209
input tokens cost.

885
00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:13.073
So it's almost like the way I understand this is

886
00:34:13.113 --> 00:34:15.036
almost like you are saying, if I'm going to be

887
00:34:15.076 --> 00:34:16.477
running always the same prompt,

888
00:34:17.018 --> 00:34:19.199
for a specific interaction, then you could

889
00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:21.200
create almost like a snapshot of that.

890
00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:22.740
So that's what gets cached.

891
00:34:23.200 --> 00:34:25.921
And then you are resuming from that session with

892
00:34:25.961 --> 00:34:27.502
maybe an additional piece of text.

893
00:34:27.622 --> 00:34:30.143
So you are not paying for all the initial input,

894
00:34:30.603 --> 00:34:33.124
which gets cached and it comes with a discount.

895
00:34:33.484 --> 00:34:36.065
And that way, you are effectively avoiding to

896
00:34:36.125 --> 00:34:38.646
resend that text over and over to different

897
00:34:38.666 --> 00:34:38.966
prompts.

898
00:34:40.687 --> 00:34:42.968
Yeah, I think if you go into Bedrock and you

899
00:34:42.988 --> 00:34:44.529
start to use all the other features,

900
00:34:44.569 --> 00:34:46.510
of course, they come with their own pricing.

901
00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:48.811
But again, today we are focusing more on the

902
00:34:48.871 --> 00:34:49.551
inference part.

903
00:34:50.051 --> 00:34:51.612
If you're interested in knowledge bases,

904
00:34:52.112 --> 00:34:54.693
understanding flows, agents, fine-tuning,

905
00:34:55.613 --> 00:34:57.134
all these different features of Bedrock,

906
00:34:57.174 --> 00:34:59.075
they will have their own pricing and different

907
00:34:59.095 --> 00:35:00.355
dimensions you need to consider.

908
00:35:00.415 --> 00:35:03.297
So go and check those out if this is something

909
00:35:03.317 --> 00:35:03.937
that interests you.

910
00:35:04.217 --> 00:35:07.318
So now I think we should try to quickly touch

911
00:35:07.618 --> 00:35:11.319
on some of the issues that might be tripping you

912
00:35:11.399 --> 00:35:11.519
up.

913
00:35:11.759 --> 00:35:12.560
What do you think, Eoin?

914
00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:15.660
Yeah, we touched on one already, which is

915
00:35:15.721 --> 00:35:16.181
throttling.

916
00:35:17.101 --> 00:35:19.102
There's quotas at two levels, requests per

917
00:35:19.142 --> 00:35:20.562
minute and tokens per minute.

918
00:35:20.962 --> 00:35:22.723
They're per model, per region, per account.

919
00:35:23.283 --> 00:35:25.864
And new accounts get shockingly low default

920
00:35:25.904 --> 00:35:28.384
quotas, like two or three requests per minute

921
00:35:28.404 --> 00:35:30.365
for some models, and that can be a real blocker.

922
00:35:31.105 --> 00:35:33.306
Even established counts can have conservative

923
00:35:33.346 --> 00:35:33.826
defaults.

924
00:35:34.366 --> 00:35:35.887
They're not giving this stuff away,

925
00:35:36.007 --> 00:35:37.948
they're really rationing it out.

926
00:35:38.468 --> 00:35:40.469
So you might get 429 throttling exception

927
00:35:40.589 --> 00:35:40.869
errors.

928
00:35:41.629 --> 00:35:44.471
Maybe plan for it by adding exponential black

929
00:35:44.531 --> 00:35:45.331
back off with jitter.

930
00:35:45.351 --> 00:35:49.853
AWS SDK can do that using adaptive mode in its

931
00:35:49.873 --> 00:35:50.213
settings.

932
00:35:50.953 --> 00:35:52.694
use cross-region inference to spread load,

933
00:35:53.214 --> 00:35:56.594
monitor with CloudWatch, and apply increase

934
00:35:56.694 --> 00:35:57.655
requests early.

935
00:35:58.595 --> 00:36:00.315
Don't wait until you're ready to go to

936
00:36:00.335 --> 00:36:00.735
production.

937
00:36:01.556 --> 00:36:03.796
And the max tokens parameter in your requests

938
00:36:04.196 --> 00:36:04.916
affects throttling.

939
00:36:04.936 --> 00:36:06.057
This is an interesting nuance.

940
00:36:06.537 --> 00:36:08.697
Bedrock reserves some tokens based on your max

941
00:36:08.717 --> 00:36:10.798
token setting up front, even if the model

942
00:36:10.858 --> 00:36:11.798
generates far fewer.

943
00:36:12.078 --> 00:36:14.619
So setting that too high can burn quota faster

944
00:36:14.639 --> 00:36:16.739
than you expect, because quota mathematics

945
00:36:16.819 --> 00:36:19.680
reserves based on what you asked for,

946
00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:20.380
not what you got.

947
00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:25.322
And some models like Claude, output tokens count

948
00:36:25.362 --> 00:36:28.482
more heavily against your quotas with a burndown

949
00:36:28.543 --> 00:36:30.423
multiplier, like 5x for Claude.

950
00:36:31.303 --> 00:36:33.784
Now, model access isn't as simple as it looks.

951
00:36:33.804 --> 00:36:34.704
This is another gotcha.

952
00:36:35.285 --> 00:36:37.625
Even though it's simplified, as we try to say,

953
00:36:37.645 --> 00:36:39.766
you still need the right IAM permissions,

954
00:36:39.906 --> 00:36:41.787
marketplace subscriptions, all of that stuff.

955
00:36:42.247 --> 00:36:44.007
Different models might be available in different

956
00:36:44.067 --> 00:36:47.789
regions, and some might only be available in US

957
00:36:47.829 --> 00:36:48.709
regions initially.

958
00:36:49.709 --> 00:36:53.510
The marketplace, we mentioned IAM permissions

959
00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:56.852
are required in order to get model access

960
00:36:57.412 --> 00:36:59.893
because of those serverless models going through

961
00:36:59.933 --> 00:37:00.593
